// The terminal package is the small interactive-console boundary shared by
// full-screen applications. It turns one existing input descriptor into an
// immediate byte stream, waits for input with a bounded timeout, queries and
// watches the terminal cell dimensions, drains queued protocol input at the
// final cleanup boundary, and restores the exact native mode saved at entry.
// Companion files own a small ANSI alternate-screen lifetime and a streaming
// key decoder. The package owns no descriptor: callers retain descriptor
// lifetime, frame construction, styling, text interpretation, and application
// event-loop policy. A Resize_Watcher temporarily owns the process SIGWINCH
// disposition on POSIX only when no application handler is already installed;
// Windows implements the same observation contract by comparing size queries.
//
// Session is an explicit move-by-convention resource. A successful begin_raw
// must be paired with restore while the input descriptor remains open. Suspend
// temporarily returns the descriptor to its saved mode; resume reapplies raw
// mode. The selected target file owns the native mode, readiness, and size-
// query/notification ABI; this common file owns target-independent lifetime,
// timeout, and error policy.
// It depends only on core/atomic, core/c_abi, core/io, core/os, and core/time.
// Draft specification: sections 5-7 and 12, plus the selected target profile.
package terminal

import core/atomic
import core/c_abi
import core/io
import core/os
import core/time

// Poll_Descriptor is the small exchange record used by common readiness policy.
// POSIX targets pass its compatible layout to poll; Windows translates it
// around WaitForSingleObject. Only native_poll_one writes returned_events.
// Hangup becomes end-of-input; all other errors become io.Error.unavailable.
Poll_Descriptor :: c struct {
    descriptor: c_abi.int,
    events: c_abi.short,
    returned_events: c_abi.short,
}

Poll_Input :: cast[c_abi.short](0x0001)
Poll_Error :: cast[c_abi.short](0x0008)
Poll_Hangup :: cast[c_abi.short](0x0010)
Poll_Invalid :: cast[c_abi.short](0x0020)

Nanoseconds_Per_Millisecond :: 1000 * 1000
Maximum_Poll_Milliseconds :: 2147483647

// Full-screen applications commonly enable terminal protocols that encode
// events as input bytes. Disabling such a protocol prevents future reports,
// but reports generated immediately beforehand may remain queued. These
// private limits make discard_pending_input observe a short quiet interval
// without letting continuous input delay cleanup indefinitely.
Discard_Input_Buffer_Size :: 4096
Discard_Input_Quiet_Period :: 50 * time.millisecond
Discard_Input_Maximum_Duration :: 250 * time.millisecond

// Native_Window_Size is the target-seam result record. POSIX writes it directly
// through TIOCGWINSZ; Windows derives the same fields from its visible console
// rectangle. Pixel dimensions establish the POSIX ABI shape but are otherwise
// ignored because text applications operate in terminal cells.
Native_Window_Size :: c struct {
    rows: c_abi.unsigned_short,
    columns: c_abi.unsigned_short,
    pixel_width: c_abi.unsigned_short,
    pixel_height: c_abi.unsigned_short,
}

// Size is one terminal cell extent. A successful native query may report zero
// for either dimension when the terminal cannot currently establish it; the
// library preserves that fact and leaves fallback policy to the application.
pub Size :: struct {
    columns: usize,
    rows: usize,
}

// Resize_Watcher_State exposes the cleanup obligation without making the
// native signal record public policy. inactive owns nothing. active retains a
// borrowed terminal descriptor and, on POSIX, the exact SIGWINCH disposition
// that end_resize_watch must restore. Only one active watcher may exist in a
// process because a POSIX signal disposition is itself process-global.
pub Resize_Watcher_State :: enum u8 {
    inactive,
    active,
}

// Resize_Watcher remembers the last successfully observed cell extent. It is
// a move-by-convention resource: keep an active value at one stable address,
// do not copy it, and call end_resize_watch before its storage or borrowed
// descriptor expires. Windows retains the same explicit lifetime even though
// its selected implementation needs no signal record.
pub Resize_Watcher :: struct {
    terminal_file: os.File,
    size: Size,
    previous_action: Native_Signal_Action,
    state: Resize_Watcher_State,
}

// These two atomics are the complete process-global coordination required by
// POSIX SIGWINCH delivery. resize_watch_claimed rejects overlapping owners.
// resize_notification_pending is written by the context-free signal callback
// and exchanged by ordinary event-loop code. Windows uses the ownership bit
// for one uniform API but does not read the pending bit.
resize_watch_claimed: atomic.Value[u32]
resize_notification_pending: atomic.Value[u32]

// resize_signal_handler is deliberately a C procedure with no Draft Context.
// A signal may arrive at any ordinary instruction, so the handler performs one
// lock-free relaxed atomic store and nothing else: no allocation, I/O, query,
// logging, or call into application code. The event loop performs every
// fallible operation later in poll_resize.
resize_signal_handler :: c proc(signal_number: c_abi.int) {
    atomic.store(&resize_notification_pending, 1, .relaxed)
}

// Session_State makes the restoration obligation and job-control transition
// explicit. inactive owns no cleanup. active means raw mode is installed.
// suspended still owns the saved mode and descriptor borrow, but has restored
// the terminal for an external shell or subprocess.
pub Session_State :: enum u8 {
    inactive,
    active,
    suspended,
}

// Session owns one saved native input mode but not the input descriptor. Its
// zero value is inactive and may be passed to begin_raw. Once non-inactive,
// copying the value would create two apparent owners of one restoration
// obligation, so callers keep it at one stable address and pass ^Session to
// every operation. Failed transitions retain their source state for retry.
pub Session :: struct {
    input: os.File,
    saved_mode: Native_Mode,
    state: Session_State,
}

// timeout_milliseconds converts the nanosecond Duration contract to poll's
// signed-millisecond ABI. Positive fractional milliseconds round upward so the
// native wait never expires earlier than requested. Large durations saturate
// instead of narrowing into a negative timeout, which poll would interpret as
// an unbounded wait. Nonpositive values are immediate polls.
timeout_milliseconds :: proc(timeout: time.Duration) -> c_abi.int {
    static_assert(size_of(Poll_Descriptor) == 8)
    static_assert(align_of(Poll_Descriptor) == 4)

    if timeout <= 0 {
        return 0
    }

    nanoseconds := cast[i64](timeout)
    milliseconds := nanoseconds / Nanoseconds_Per_Millisecond
    if nanoseconds % Nanoseconds_Per_Millisecond != 0 {
        milliseconds += 1
    }
    if milliseconds > Maximum_Poll_Milliseconds {
        return cast[c_abi.int](Maximum_Poll_Milliseconds)
    }
    return cast[c_abi.int](milliseconds)
}

// apply_raw_mode derives the platform's conventional raw mode from a borrowed
// saved mode and applies it immediately. It changes no Session state: callers
// publish a transition only after the native operation succeeds.
apply_raw_mode :: proc(
    input: os.File,
    saved_mode: ^Native_Mode,
) -> bool {
    raw_mode := saved_mode^
    native_make_raw(&raw_mode)
    return native_set_mode(
        input.descriptor,
        Native_Apply_Now,
        &raw_mode,
    ) == 0
}

// begin_raw saves input's complete native terminal mode, derives the platform's
// conventional raw mode, and applies it immediately. Raw mode
// disables canonical line buffering, echo, signal-generating control keys, and
// byte transformations; Ctrl-C therefore arrives as byte 3 for application
// policy rather than terminating before normal cleanup can run.
//
// session must be non-nil, inactive, and kept at a stable address until a
// successful restore. input is borrowed and must remain open. No Session field
// changes unless both native mode operations succeed. An invalid descriptor or
// repeated begin returns io.Error.invalid; native failure returns unavailable.
pub begin_raw :: proc(session: ^Session, input: os.File) -> io.Error {
    if session^.state != .inactive || input.descriptor < 0 {
        return .invalid
    }

    saved_mode: Native_Mode = ---
    if native_get_mode(input.descriptor, &saved_mode) != 0 {
        return .unavailable
    }

    if !apply_raw_mode(input, &saved_mode) {
        return .unavailable
    }

    session^.input = input
    session^.saved_mode = saved_mode
    session^.state = .active
    return .none
}

// suspend temporarily restores the exact mode saved by begin_raw without
// releasing the Session's restoration obligation or descriptor borrow. It is
// idempotent while already suspended and fails for an inactive Session. Native
// failure leaves the Session active so cleanup or another attempt remains
// possible. Applications call this before yielding terminal control; signal
// installation and process suspension remain explicit application policy.
pub suspend :: proc(session: ^Session) -> bool {
    if session^.state == .inactive {
        return false
    }
    if session^.state == .suspended {
        return true
    }
    if native_set_mode(
        session^.input.descriptor,
        Native_Apply_Now,
        &session^.saved_mode,
    ) != 0 {
        return false
    }
    session^.state = .suspended
    return true
}

// resume reapplies raw mode after a successful suspend. It is idempotent while
// already active and fails for an inactive Session. Native failure leaves the
// Session suspended, so the external terminal-facing mode remains authoritative
// and the owner may retry without losing its saved mode.
pub resume :: proc(session: ^Session) -> bool {
    if session^.state == .inactive {
        return false
    }
    if session^.state == .active {
        return true
    }
    if !apply_raw_mode(session^.input, &session^.saved_mode) {
        return false
    }
    session^.state = .active
    return true
}

// restore reapplies the exact mode saved by begin_raw. It is idempotent for an
// inactive Session. A suspended Session is already in the saved mode and ends
// its obligation without another native call. A native failure from active
// state retains that state and the saved mode so the owner can report or retry;
// success never closes the borrowed descriptor.
pub restore :: proc(session: ^Session) -> bool {
    if session^.state == .inactive {
        return true
    }
    if session^.state == .suspended {
        session^.state = .inactive
        return true
    }
    if native_set_mode(
        session^.input.descriptor,
        Native_Apply_Now,
        &session^.saved_mode,
    ) != 0 {
        return false
    }
    session^.state = .inactive
    return true
}

// query_size asks one borrowed terminal descriptor for its current rows and
// columns. The target operation writes the exchange record synchronously and
// retains no pointer. Invalid descriptors
// return .invalid before native code. Native failure returns .unavailable and a
// zero Size. Successful zero dimensions are preserved as a valid observation.
pub query_size :: proc(terminal_file: os.File) -> (Size, io.Error) {
    static_assert(size_of(Native_Window_Size) == 8)
    static_assert(align_of(Native_Window_Size) == 2)

    if terminal_file.descriptor < 0 {
        empty: Size
        return (empty, .invalid)
    }
    native_size: Native_Window_Size = ---
    if native_query_size(terminal_file.descriptor, &native_size) != 0 {
        empty: Size
        return (empty, .unavailable)
    }
    return (
        Size{
            columns = cast[usize](native_size.columns),
            rows = cast[usize](native_size.rows),
        },
        .none,
    )
}

// begin_resize_watch establishes one explicit size-change observation owner
// and returns its initial size. watcher must be inactive and terminal_file must
// be a valid terminal descriptor. On POSIX, native_begin_resize_watch refuses
// to replace any non-default SIGWINCH disposition; on every target a second
// active watcher returns unavailable rather than sharing one notification bit.
//
// The initial query happens before the process-wide claim. Once native setup
// succeeds, pending is set so the first poll repeats the query and closes the
// small race in which the terminal changes between that query and handler
// installation. Failure publishes no watcher state or cleanup obligation.
pub begin_resize_watch :: proc(
    watcher: ^Resize_Watcher,
    terminal_file: os.File,
) -> (Size, io.Error) {
    empty: Size
    if watcher^.state != .inactive || terminal_file.descriptor < 0 {
        return (empty, .invalid)
    }

    (initial, query_error) := query_size(terminal_file)
    if query_error != .none {
        return (empty, query_error)
    }

    expected: u32
    if !atomic.compare_exchange(
        &resize_watch_claimed,
        &expected,
        1,
        .acquire_release,
        .acquire,
    ) {
        return (empty, .unavailable)
    }

    previous: Native_Signal_Action
    if !native_begin_resize_watch(&previous) {
        atomic.store(&resize_watch_claimed, 0, .release)
        return (empty, .unavailable)
    }

    watcher^.terminal_file = terminal_file
    watcher^.size = initial
    watcher^.previous_action = previous
    watcher^.state = .active
    atomic.store(&resize_notification_pending, 1, .relaxed)
    return (initial, .none)
}

// poll_resize consumes at most one coalesced size notification. POSIX avoids a
// native size query when no SIGWINCH arrived. Windows has no matching signal,
// so its selected constant makes each poll compare one synchronous query. A
// successful unchanged observation returns the remembered Size and false.
//
// Query failure preserves the old size. POSIX republishes pending so a later
// call retries without waiting for another signal. No result borrows watcher
// storage, and successful change observations follow native delivery order
// only up to normal signal coalescing.
pub poll_resize :: proc(
    watcher: ^Resize_Watcher,
) -> (Size, bool, io.Error) {
    if watcher^.state != .active {
        empty: Size
        return (empty, false, .invalid)
    }

    when Native_Resize_Uses_Notifications {
        pending := atomic.exchange(
            &resize_notification_pending,
            0,
            .relaxed,
        )
        if pending == 0 {
            return (watcher^.size, false, .none)
        }
    }

    (observed, error) := query_size(watcher^.terminal_file)
    if error != .none {
        when Native_Resize_Uses_Notifications {
            atomic.store(&resize_notification_pending, 1, .relaxed)
        }
        return (watcher^.size, false, error)
    }

    changed := observed.columns != watcher^.size.columns ||
        observed.rows != watcher^.size.rows
    watcher^.size = observed
    return (observed, changed, .none)
}

// end_resize_watch restores the exact native notification disposition captured
// by begin and releases the process-wide claim. It is idempotent while
// inactive. A native restoration failure leaves watcher active and claimed so
// the same owner can report or retry instead of admitting a second owner while
// the first handler may still be installed.
pub end_resize_watch :: proc(watcher: ^Resize_Watcher) -> bool {
    if watcher^.state == .inactive {
        return true
    }
    if !native_end_resize_watch(&watcher^.previous_action) {
        return false
    }

    watcher^.state = .inactive
    atomic.store(&resize_notification_pending, 0, .relaxed)
    atomic.store(&resize_watch_claimed, 0, .release)
    return true
}

// read waits for input using timeout rounded upward to native millisecond
// resolution, then performs one ordinary os.read into caller-owned storage. A
// zero timeout polls immediately; any positive sub-millisecond duration waits
// one millisecond. A clean timeout returns (0, .none), while terminal hangup
// returns .end_of_input. The call neither allocates nor retains destination. It
// requires an active Session and returns .invalid while inactive or suspended.
// Empty destination succeeds immediately after validating resource state.
pub read :: proc(
    session: ^Session,
    destination: []u8,
    timeout: time.Duration,
) -> (usize, io.Error) {
    if session^.state != .active {
        return (0, .invalid)
    }
    if len(destination) == 0 {
        return (0, .none)
    }

    descriptor := Poll_Descriptor{
        descriptor = session^.input.descriptor,
        events = Poll_Input,
    }
    ready := native_poll_one(&descriptor, timeout_milliseconds(timeout))
    if ready < 0 {
        // POSIX poll returns EINTR when SIGWINCH arrives because the installed
        // action deliberately omits SA_RESTART. Treat only our pending resize
        // as an ordinary empty turn; every unrelated interruption/native error
        // remains unavailable. poll_resize consumes the flag on the next turn.
        when Native_Resize_Uses_Notifications {
            if atomic.load(&resize_notification_pending, .relaxed) != 0 {
                return (0, .none)
            }
        }
        return (0, .unavailable)
    }
    if ready == 0 {
        return (0, .none)
    }

    // A peer may close after readiness is reported. os.read remains the
    // authority for that race and translates a zero-byte native read to EOF.
    if descriptor.returned_events & Poll_Input != 0 {
        return os.read(session^.input, destination)
    }
    if descriptor.returned_events & Poll_Hangup != 0 {
        return (0, .end_of_input)
    }
    if descriptor.returned_events & (Poll_Error | Poll_Invalid) != 0 {
        return (0, .unavailable)
    }

    // The target promised one ready descriptor but supplied no event understood by
    // the selected contract. Treat that mismatch as native unavailability
    // rather than spinning the application event loop without progress.
    return (0, .unavailable)
}

// discard_pending_input consumes terminal bytes until the active Session has
// remained quiet for a short bounded interval. It exists for one destructive
// cleanup boundary: after an application disables a byte-producing terminal
// protocol such as mouse reporting, and before it restores cooked input for
// the parent shell. A zero-time poll alone is insufficient because the
// terminal emulator may still be delivering reports generated just before the
// disable sequence was processed.
//
// The operation retains no bytes, allocates nothing, and leaves the Session
// active. Hangup means there is no input to leak and succeeds. Invalid Session
// state and native read failures propagate normally. Continuous input is
// bounded by Discard_Input_Maximum_Duration and returns .unavailable instead
// of making terminal restoration wait forever. Applications must still
// attempt every later restoration step after any error.
pub discard_pending_input :: proc(session: ^Session) -> io.Error {
    if session^.state != .active {
        return .invalid
    }

    storage: [Discard_Input_Buffer_Size]u8
    started := time.monotonic_now()
    quiet_since := started
    for {
        now := time.monotonic_now()
        if now - started >= Discard_Input_Maximum_Duration {
            return .unavailable
        }
        if now - quiet_since >= Discard_Input_Quiet_Period {
            return .none
        }

        (count, error) := read(
            session,
            storage[:],
            Discard_Input_Quiet_Period,
        )
        if error == .end_of_input {
            return .none
        }
        if error != .none {
            return error
        }
        if count != 0 {
            quiet_since = time.monotonic_now()
        }
    }

    // The unconditional loop exits only through the returns above. Draft's
    // structural return checker intentionally does not prove that fact.
    return .unavailable
}
